1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,643
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,663 --> 00:00:08,020
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:13,955
This is Lecture 12, automatic sewing units and flexible cells.

4
00:00:14,355 --> 00:00:20,749
A garment passes through anywhere from a dozen to several hundred operations.

5
00:00:20,769 --> 00:00:25,747
Today we step from a single machine out onto the shop floor,

6
00:00:25,767 --> 00:00:30,162
and ask how to place the machines and share the work.

7
00:00:31,682 --> 00:00:33,222
The shop-floor problem.

8
00:00:33,622 --> 00:00:42,042
A trouser factory has put several automatic pocket welters in a U.

9
00:00:42,442 --> 00:00:47,386
Stopwatch timing: unloading the finished part and loading the panel,

10
00:00:47,406 --> 00:00:52,935
welt strip and flap takes about 5 seconds; the machine's automatic locating,

11
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:56,220
sewing, corner cutting and stacking about 12;

12
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,578
walking between neighbouring machines about 2.

13
00:00:59,978 --> 00:01:04,541
The supervisor wants to know how many machines one operator should tend.

14
00:01:04,941 --> 00:01:11,305
And there are two ways to raise output: give the operator one more machine,

15
00:01:11,325 --> 00:01:16,752
or invest in a loading fixture that cuts loading to 3.5 seconds.

16
00:01:17,152 --> 00:01:18,012
Which pays?

17
00:01:19,532 --> 00:01:20,891
Automatic sewing units.

18
00:01:22,411 --> 00:01:27,191
Take one unit: the automatic pocket welter.

19
00:01:27,591 --> 00:01:34,140
The operator lays down the panel, flap and welt strip; the clamp drops,

20
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:41,448
sensors read the flap position and work out where the seams and corner cuts go;

21
00:01:41,468 --> 00:01:47,369
folding plates fold the welt, two needles sew both rows at once,

22
00:01:47,389 --> 00:01:53,938
a centre knife slits between them, corner knives cut the Y at each end,

23
00:01:53,958 --> 00:01:57,731
and the part is flipped onto the stacker.

24
00:01:58,131 --> 00:02:02,962
One model sews 3000 stitches a minute,

25
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:12,791
and the catalogue gives 1600 to 2300 pieces a shift, 12.5 to 18 seconds each.

26
00:02:13,191 --> 00:02:18,851
Its value lies in fixtures and program, not sewing speed.

27
00:02:20,371 --> 00:02:23,031
Multi-machine tending: how many machines.

28
00:02:24,551 --> 00:02:31,842
One unit's cycle is a plus m: the operator loads for a, the machine runs for m.

29
00:02:32,242 --> 00:02:39,350
Tending N machines, the operator's round takes N times a plus w,

30
00:02:39,370 --> 00:02:44,138
while each machine needs at least a plus m.

31
00:02:44,538 --> 00:02:48,195
The larger of the two is the cycle time.

32
00:02:48,595 --> 00:02:53,900
The balance point N-star is a plus m over a plus w.

33
00:02:54,300 --> 00:03:00,280
Below N-star the operator is idle, waiting for machines; above it,

34
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:03,916
finished machines wait for the operator.

35
00:03:04,316 --> 00:03:09,513
On the right, one cycle with two machines and with three.

36
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:16,900
This is the floor view from Virtual Lab 12-1.

37
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:23,096
The blue dot is the operator walking between two automatic pocket welters.

38
00:03:23,496 --> 00:03:29,021
At each one she unloads the finished part, loads the next, and starts it;

39
00:03:29,041 --> 00:03:32,363
the bar shows the machine sewing on its own.

40
00:03:32,763 --> 00:03:38,309
With two machines, by the time she has gone round and come back,

41
00:03:38,329 --> 00:03:44,223
the first machine hasn't finished, so she waits a few seconds there.

42
00:03:44,623 --> 00:03:47,323
That's the operator waiting for the machine.

43
00:03:48,843 --> 00:03:54,644
Watch the Gantt chart switching every 7 seconds between two and three machines.

44
00:03:55,044 --> 00:04:00,593
With two, the operator's round is 14 seconds and the machine cycle 17,

45
00:04:00,613 --> 00:04:06,400
so the machines set the pace and the operator waits 3 seconds each round,

46
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:09,104
busy about 82 percent of the time.

47
00:04:09,504 --> 00:04:16,097
With three, the round is 21 seconds, longer than the machine cycle,

48
00:04:16,117 --> 00:04:23,401
so the operator sets the pace and each machine waits 4 seconds a cycle for

49
00:04:23,421 --> 00:04:24,684
the operator.

50
00:04:26,204 --> 00:04:28,064
Put the numbers in.

51
00:04:28,464 --> 00:04:34,124
The balance point is 17 over 7, about 2.43.

52
00:04:34,524 --> 00:04:47,080
One machine gives about 212 pieces an hour; two about 424; three about 514;

53
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:51,104
four still 514, no more.

54
00:04:51,504 --> 00:04:59,955
Per machine, two give 212 each and three only 171.

55
00:05:00,355 --> 00:05:07,639
So if machines are expensive, tend fewer, two; if labour is expensive,

56
00:05:07,659 --> 00:05:09,412
tend more, three.

57
00:05:10,932 --> 00:05:16,232
Hanger systems: who gets the carrier, how many.

58
00:05:17,752 --> 00:05:23,852
A hanger system changes the unit of production from a bundle to a single piece:

59
00:05:23,872 --> 00:05:26,873
each cut part rides on its own carrier.

60
00:05:27,273 --> 00:05:31,139
The main-rail chain circulates the carriers,

61
00:05:31,159 --> 00:05:35,379
a reader identifies each one by barcode or RFID,

62
00:05:35,399 --> 00:05:40,590
and the system decides whether to switch it into a station.

63
00:05:40,990 --> 00:05:45,765
The spur is that station's buffer; when the operator presses done,

64
00:05:45,785 --> 00:05:51,650
a lift returns the carrier to the main rail and records who finished it and when.

65
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:57,732
Main-rail capacity is chain speed over spacing:

66
00:05:57,752 --> 00:06:05,860
at 12 metres a minute and 0.3-metre spacing, 2400 carriers an hour,

67
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:08,529
rarely the bottleneck.

68
00:06:10,049 --> 00:06:13,269
Who gets the carrier after each operation?

69
00:06:13,669 --> 00:06:21,186
The example is a T-shirt with 6 operations totalling 260 standard seconds and

70
00:06:21,206 --> 00:06:22,262
10 workers,

71
00:06:22,282 --> 00:06:29,408
each able to do one or two operations, with efficiencies from 0.8 to 1.1.

72
00:06:29,808 --> 00:06:36,645
Fixed dispatching sends work only to the worker whose main job it is;

73
00:06:36,665 --> 00:06:43,602
shortest queue sends it to a qualified worker with the fewest waiting;

74
00:06:43,622 --> 00:06:48,373
earliest finish estimates who will finish first.

75
00:06:48,773 --> 00:06:58,433
Simulated over 4 hours: about 111, 126 and 124 pieces an hour.

76
00:06:58,833 --> 00:07:06,236
With fixed stations the bottleneck is sleeve hemming, done only by worker 8,

77
00:07:06,256 --> 00:07:13,074
capped at about 113; dynamic dispatching lets worker 7 help when idle.

78
00:07:14,593 --> 00:07:17,600
How many carriers should be on the line?

79
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:25,058
Too few and workers run out of work; beyond about 20 to 25,

80
00:07:25,078 --> 00:07:27,457
output stops rising.

81
00:07:27,857 --> 00:07:33,757
Little's law: WIP equals throughput times average throughput time.

82
00:07:34,157 --> 00:07:39,178
Under earliest finish the line makes 124 pieces an hour,

83
00:07:39,198 --> 00:07:44,130
and a garment spends on average 0.31 hours on the line;

84
00:07:44,150 --> 00:07:50,251
multiply and you get about 38, matching the 40 carriers on the line.

85
00:07:50,651 --> 00:07:57,231
Cut the limit to 20 and output barely changes, but the throughput time halves.

86
00:07:57,631 --> 00:08:01,491
So set the limit where output has just levelled off.

87
00:08:03,011 --> 00:08:05,078
U-cells and bucket brigades.

88
00:08:06,598 --> 00:08:13,963
A flexible U-cell places a dozen or so machines close together in a U,

89
00:08:13,983 --> 00:08:17,866
with entry and exit on the same side.

90
00:08:18,266 --> 00:08:21,908
Each part goes straight to the next machine,

91
00:08:21,928 --> 00:08:28,232
so the only work in process is what's in people's hands and on the machines.

92
00:08:28,632 --> 00:08:34,613
Operators are multi-skilled: whoever is free helps the next operation,

93
00:08:34,633 --> 00:08:39,156
and one person can cover both the first and the last.

94
00:08:39,556 --> 00:08:43,554
A style change means moving just a few machines,

95
00:08:43,574 --> 00:08:47,656
which suits small orders of a few hundred pieces.

96
00:08:49,176 --> 00:08:53,881
A bucket brigade has one rule: when the last worker finishes a piece,

97
00:08:53,901 --> 00:08:58,469
they walk back and take over the piece from the worker before them,

98
00:08:58,489 --> 00:09:02,646
who walks back in turn, and nobody may pass the person ahead.

99
00:09:03,046 --> 00:09:10,322
Ordered from slowest to fastest, the hand-off points settle by themselves.

100
00:09:10,722 --> 00:09:16,708
At the top, three workers move along the job from 0 to 1; below,

101
00:09:16,728 --> 00:09:23,652
the hand-off position for each garment, quickly settling at 0.17 and 0.50,

102
00:09:23,672 --> 00:09:26,655
that is, one sixth and one half.

103
00:09:28,175 --> 00:09:30,715
Why one sixth and one half?

104
00:09:31,115 --> 00:09:39,052
The hand-offs converge to x-i equals the sum of the first i speeds over the

105
00:09:39,072 --> 00:09:40,962
sum of all speeds:

106
00:09:40,982 --> 00:09:46,055
0.5 over 3 is one sixth, 1.5 over 3 is one half.

107
00:09:46,455 --> 00:09:51,184
Each person's share is proportional to their speed,

108
00:09:51,204 --> 00:09:55,934
and output equals the sum of the three speeds, 3.0.

109
00:09:56,334 --> 00:10:03,994
If each did a fixed third, the slowest would set the pace: only 1.5.

110
00:10:04,394 --> 00:10:09,334
The beauty is that nobody needs to know the speeds in advance;

111
00:10:09,354 --> 00:10:12,454
the work balances without a supervisor.

112
00:10:13,974 --> 00:10:18,373
Finally, let's carry these rules over to robots.

113
00:10:18,773 --> 00:10:23,699
A cobot loading several CNC machines or automatic sewing units is

114
00:10:23,719 --> 00:10:25,373
multi-machine tending:

115
00:10:25,393 --> 00:10:29,177
its round time is compared with the machine cycle,

116
00:10:29,197 --> 00:10:32,829
and the balance-point formula applies unchanged.

117
00:10:33,229 --> 00:10:37,609
A robot's loading and travel times vary little,

118
00:10:37,629 --> 00:10:42,477
so less slack is needed between it and the machines,

119
00:10:42,497 --> 00:10:47,907
and it can work closer to the balance point than a person.

120
00:10:48,307 --> 00:10:56,201
Hanger dispatching rules reappear almost unchanged in AGV and AMR fleet dispatching:

121
00:10:56,221 --> 00:11:04,021
a job goes to the vehicle with the shortest queue or the earliest predicted finish,

122
00:11:04,041 --> 00:11:08,731
pallets in transit play the role of the WIP limit,

123
00:11:08,751 --> 00:11:12,689
and Little's law sets the throughput time.

124
00:11:14,208 --> 00:11:16,757
Little's law at the coffee shop:

125
00:11:16,777 --> 00:11:22,134
ten people are queuing and the baristas turn out two cups a minute,

126
00:11:22,154 --> 00:11:26,228
so you'll wait about ten divided by two, 5 minutes.

127
00:11:26,628 --> 00:11:32,444
WIP equals throughput times throughput time.

128
00:11:32,844 --> 00:11:39,886
With the same staff, a longer queue means a longer wait, but not faster coffee.

129
00:11:40,286 --> 00:11:46,217
Hanging more carriers on a hanger line is the same: the same output,

130
00:11:46,237 --> 00:11:48,404
a longer throughput time.

131
00:11:49,924 --> 00:11:51,144
Back to the start.

132
00:11:51,544 --> 00:11:57,204
The balance point is 17 over 7, about 2.43.

133
00:11:57,604 --> 00:12:01,779
Two machines give about 424 pieces an hour,

134
00:12:01,799 --> 00:12:07,924
with the operator idle 3 seconds a round; three give about 514,

135
00:12:07,944 --> 00:12:11,729
each machine waiting 4 seconds a cycle.

136
00:12:12,129 --> 00:12:19,004
If machines are dear, tend two; if labour is dear, tend three.

137
00:12:19,404 --> 00:12:27,575
As for the two improvements: one more machine adds 91, the fixture alone 41,

138
00:12:27,595 --> 00:12:34,904
and both together reach about 655, a gain of 231, more than the sum.

139
00:12:35,304 --> 00:12:40,752
Shorter loading pushes the balance point to about 2.8,

140
00:12:40,772 --> 00:12:46,524
so with three machines each waits only 1 seconds a cycle.

141
00:12:48,044 --> 00:12:51,631
Now Virtual lab 12-1, multi-machine tending.

142
00:12:51,651 --> 00:12:56,632
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself. The results,

143
00:12:56,652 --> 00:13:00,567
the output curve and the tasks are blurred here,

144
00:13:00,587 --> 00:13:04,338
so you can find them on your own. On the left,

145
00:13:04,358 --> 00:13:08,519
the floor view is on top and the Gantt chart below.

146
00:13:08,919 --> 00:13:14,253
Task one: work out the balance point with the formula and enter it to check.

147
00:13:14,273 --> 00:13:18,901
Then drag the number of machines from 1 to 4. Here we go to three.

148
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:20,240
On the Gantt chart,

149
00:13:20,260 --> 00:13:25,452
watch the waiting blocks move from the operator's row to the machine rows,

150
00:13:25,472 --> 00:13:29,819
and note who waits for whom, and for how many seconds a round.

151
00:13:30,219 --> 00:13:35,031
Task two: set the variation to 0.3. Read how much two,

152
00:13:35,051 --> 00:13:41,474
three and four machines lose, and think about which loses most, and why.

153
00:13:41,874 --> 00:13:42,835
Task three:

154
00:13:42,855 --> 00:13:49,164
set the variation back to zero and cut the loading time to 3.5 seconds.

155
00:13:49,184 --> 00:13:53,264
Check the three outputs from the answer slide,

156
00:13:53,284 --> 00:14:00,307
and on the Gantt chart see how long each of three machines still waits a round.

157
00:14:01,827 --> 00:14:07,364
Next, Virtual lab 12-2, hanger dispatching and bucket brigades.

158
00:14:07,384 --> 00:14:12,216
Page one is a top view of the hanger line. The results,

159
00:14:12,236 --> 00:14:16,802
the output curve and the tasks are blurred here too.

160
00:14:17,202 --> 00:14:21,937
Task one: switch the dispatching rule between fixed stations,

161
00:14:21,957 --> 00:14:27,316
shortest queue and earliest finish. Here we switch to shortest queue.

162
00:14:27,336 --> 00:14:32,382
Watch the carriers flow to the stations with the shortest queues.

163
00:14:32,782 --> 00:14:39,815
Then take worker 5 off the line for the second hour and run each rule again.

164
00:14:39,835 --> 00:14:46,217
Record the output of all six cases, and check each with Little's law.

165
00:14:46,617 --> 00:14:49,192
Task two: with nobody away,

166
00:14:49,212 --> 00:14:54,862
switch the rule to earliest finish and lower the WIP limit.

167
00:14:54,882 --> 00:15:01,685
Find the smallest limit that still gives 98 percent of the best output,

168
00:15:01,705 --> 00:15:05,337
and enter it in the task box to check.

169
00:15:05,737 --> 00:15:10,019
Task three is on page two, the bucket brigade.

170
00:15:10,039 --> 00:15:17,127
First order the three workers from slowest to fastest and watch the hand-off

171
00:15:17,147 --> 00:15:18,437
points settle.

172
00:15:18,837 --> 00:15:21,022
Then switch to fastest first.

173
00:15:21,042 --> 00:15:25,966
Watch what happens when a fast worker catches up with a slow one,

174
00:15:25,986 --> 00:15:29,084
and compare the output of the two orders.

