202
00:17:42,915 --> 00:17:50,399
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example, loading takes 6 seconds,

203
00:17:50,419 --> 00:17:56,211
sewing 15, and the walk 1.5. What is the balance point?

204
00:17:56,611 --> 00:18:01,908
Or, a hanger line makes 120 pieces an hour with 50 carriers on it.

205
00:18:01,928 --> 00:18:07,628
How many minutes does a garment spend on the line? And a true-or-false:

206
00:18:07,648 --> 00:18:13,992
above the balance point, the operator has idle time every round. True or false?

207
00:18:14,392 --> 00:18:19,132
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

208
00:18:20,652 --> 00:18:22,432
Three things to remember.

209
00:18:22,832 --> 00:18:25,376
One: in multi-machine tending,

210
00:18:25,396 --> 00:18:31,360
the cycle is the larger of the operator's round and the machine cycle,

211
00:18:31,380 --> 00:18:38,028
the balance point is a plus m over a plus w, and variation hurts most near it.

212
00:18:38,428 --> 00:18:38,791
Two:

213
00:18:38,811 --> 00:18:46,832
hanger systems should dispatch dynamically and cap WIP where output just levels off;

214
00:18:46,852 --> 00:18:51,045
WIP equals throughput times throughput time.

215
00:18:51,445 --> 00:18:51,921
Three:

216
00:18:51,941 --> 00:18:58,786
a bucket brigade ordered slowest to fastest shares the work in proportion to speed,

217
00:18:58,806 --> 00:19:02,095
and output equals the sum of the speeds.

